-- allium: 3
-- db.allium
--
-- The processing model of a single database within an XTDB node: transaction submission → log processing → block flushing → query.
-- Compaction job selection and the merge algorithm are compaction.allium's; the compaction message flow is here.
--
-- Scope: one database (e.g. "xtdb" or a secondary).
-- External: the multi-database orchestration layer (attach/detach), and leader election (log-processor-lifecycle.allium).
--
-- Source and replica are always separate logs — an invariant, not a convention.
-- One node holds one role per database at a time, leading or following, and one live index shared between them.
-- A leader feeds that index from its own replica-log READ-BACK; a follower feeds it from the replica messages it reads.
-- The compactor writes L1+ tries to the source log, and the leader forwards them to the replica log.
--
-- Leader pipelining — resolve, append, read back, apply:
--   The leader applies its OWN writes only once it has read them BACK off its own replica log at its own TERM — Raft's ReadIndex.
--   That read-back IS the fence.
--   Four stages: RESOLVE a transaction against what the leader already holds and stage it; APPEND it to the replica log, stamped with the term; READ BACK the leader's own replica log from the position its promotion claimed; APPLY what the fence admits.
--   Resolution and apply never interleave, so a transaction is never half-applied.
--   Staging, ownership and the durability handles: live-index.allium. The role lifecycle, the term and its epoch: log-processor-lifecycle.allium.
--
-- Two heads, two homes — callers choose:
--   RESOLVED head (on the resolver): the highest transaction resolved, and so staged.
--     Drives resolution — the next external-source tx_id, system-time smoothing, read-your-writes.
--   DURABLE head (on the live index): the highest transaction applied, i.e. read back and imported. The QUERY BASIS.
--   Resolved leads; durable lags by everything staged but not yet applied.
--
-- Two source watermarks, and the tell that they differ:
--   The leader's RESOLVE-side watermark is what gets STAMPED onto each replica record.
--   The DURABLE one lives on the watchers and advances only on apply.
--   Stamping from the watchers instead would regress their monotonicity, because a source transaction resolved behind an external-source transaction applies first and pushes the watermark past the stale stamp.
--
-- A follower has no resolver and no read-back: every message it imports is already durable, so it imports straight into the durable live index and advances the watchers as it goes.
--
-- A query MUST NEVER see a transaction the leader has not read back: external snapshots open at the durable head.
--
-- One leader kind, not two.
--   An external source is an OPTIONAL COLLABORATOR of the leader rather than a separate processor: flush, tries and block coordination and the whole resolve → append → read back → apply pipeline are identical either way.
--   The two differ only in where a transaction enters and how its tx_id is assigned.
--     - No external source: client-driven. The leader subscribes to the source log, resolves transaction messages, and takes each tx_id from the source-log position.
--     - External source configured: transactions arrive through the indexing callback, and tx_id is assigned off the RESOLVED head.
--       A client submit is rejected with xtdb/submit-tx-to-external-source-db.
--       The source log is still consumed, for flush, tries and block coordination only.

use "./trie-cat.allium" as trie_cat
use "./compaction.allium" as compaction

---- Given

given {
    database: Database
    log_processor: LogProcessor
    tx_resolver: TxResolver?           -- leader-only; absent while following
    live_index: LiveIndex              -- shared across both roles
    watchers: Watchers                 -- correlates source msg_ids and tx_ids with waiting clients
    table_catalog: TableCatalog
    compactor: Compactor               -- a peer of the log processor, not a part of it
    storage_format: StorageFormat      -- this node's current storage format
}

---- External Entities

external entity Client {
    -- The party submitting transactions.
}

external entity Throwable {
    -- An error. Opaque here: the abort cause on a resolved transaction or a caller-fault database operation, surfaced to the client but never inspected by this spec's rules.
}

external entity Log {
    -- An ordered, append-only message stream.
    -- Two separate instances per database: source_log and replica_log, which MUST be distinct logs rather than aliases for one.
}

external entity ObjectStore {
    -- Durable key-value storage for blocks, tries and per-table block files.
}

external entity Compactor {
    -- Compacts a database's tries. A peer of the log processor rather than a part of it: it is signalled at block boundaries and otherwise runs on its own.
    -- See: compaction.allium.
}

external entity ExternalSource {
    -- A system outside XTDB that feeds a database by change data capture, e.g. Postgres logical replication.
    -- On assignment it is handed an indexing callback and an opaque resume token to start after, and feeds transactions in directly.
    -- An external-source database assigns its own monotonically increasing tx_id off the RESOLVED head rather than taking it from the source-log position.
    -- A client submit is rejected for such a database: transactions enter only through the source.
}

---- Entities

entity LiveIndex {
    -- See: live-index.allium. Shared between this node's two roles.
    --
    -- latest_completed_tx — DURABLE head / QUERY BASIS: the highest transaction in the durable live tables, and what external snapshots open at.
    --   On a leader it is advanced by apply on READ-BACK, so it lags the resolved head by everything staged but not yet read back.
    --   On a follower it is advanced by the import itself, with no lag, every message a follower imports being already durable.
    -- The RESOLVED head is NOT a field here: on a leader it lives on the resolver, and the live index stays resolver-agnostic.
    latest_completed_tx: TransactionKey?
    is_full: Boolean

    -- Rows already applied into the currently-open block.
    -- Seeds a new leader's block gauge, so a leader inheriting a partially-filled block cuts it where the previous one would have.
    block_row_count: Integer

    -- Derived
    has_data: latest_completed_tx != null
}

entity Database {
    source_log: Log
    replica_log: Log

    -- The configured external source, if any.
    -- This — not this node's role — is what rejects a client submit: the source log is shared, so any node's submit path has to reach the same decision whether or not it happens to be the leader.
    -- Opened and closed with the database on each node that could lead it, so one source spans every leader term that node serves.
    -- A node that indexes nothing, or holds the database read-only, can never lead and opens no source.
    external_source: ExternalSource?

    invariant DistinctLogs {
        source_log != replica_log
    }
}

entity LogProcessor {
    -- This node's participation in one database. Starts following, and is always in exactly one role.
    -- log-processor-lifecycle.allium owns the role state machine and the transitions; `mode` here is the coarser view this spec's rules need.
    --
    -- latest_replica_msg_id: the position this node's single replica read has reached — the last record it finished with, applied or discarded.
    --   That read is its sole writer and advances it once per record, so the position spans both roles rather than being tracked by each.
    --   A role that ends mid-apply therefore leaves it where it was, and that record is offered again to whatever replaces the role.
    --   Seeded at open from the last persisted block's boundary position, and lives for as long as the database is open on this node.
    --   The read is started once and nothing restarts it, so once it stops this node consumes no further replica records, and a promotion awaiting a position is refused rather than answered.
    --
    -- The SOURCE-log watermark is deliberately NOT here.
    --   The durable one lives on the watchers, advanced on apply by whichever role is live; the leader additionally keeps a resolve-side one, ahead of it, on the Leader variant.
    --   Two watermarks that lead and lag each other are two fields, not one.
    --
    -- max_leader_term: the READ-SIDE TERM FENCE — the highest leader term seen so far.
    --   A record BELOW it was written by a superseded leader and is discarded (see Fencing).
    --   Folded and tested by this node's replica read, before either role acts on the record: records are read one at a time and awaited, so the fence has a single caller at any moment.
    --   Seeded from the last persisted block's term so that it survives restart.
    --   Scoped to the database on this node for its whole life rather than to a role — log-processor-lifecycle.allium's LeaderTerm carries why.
    --
    -- pending_block: the block cut and not yet adopted, holding whatever arrived behind its boundary — absent unless one is in flight.
    --   Whichever role is live holds it: a follower that read the boundary, or a leader that produced the block and is waiting to read its own confirmation back.
    --   A block produced and not adopted MUST be handed to the role that replaces the one holding it, in either direction.
    --   The adopt is what moves this node's catalog past the block, so the confirmation does not read as stale; dropped, it reaches a role with no block to match and stops this node's replica read.
    mode: Leader | Follower
    latest_replica_msg_id: MessageId
    max_leader_term: Integer
    pending_block: PendingBlock?
}

variant Leader : LogProcessor {
    -- The sole writer of this database's replica log for its term.
    -- Resolves source-log messages and external-source transactions, appends them stamped with its term, reads them back and applies them.
    -- Also owns the block cycle, and the trie and block garbage collectors.
    --
    -- A promotion's inherited block is produced through this role, before it reads its first source message, and adopted once it reads an upload of that block back.
    --   The boundary the outgoing leader cut is uploaded under the INCOMING term — see The block cycle.
    --   The records the follower was holding drain at the adopt, each having met the fence at its own position in the log on the way in.
    --
    -- external_source is present iff the database has one configured; it makes this leader externally fed rather than client-driven.
    external_source: ExternalSource?

    -- This leader's fencing term: the elector's counter packed under an operator-declared epoch (see log-processor-lifecycle.allium).
    -- Stamped onto every replica record this term writes, and checked on read-back.
    leader_term: Integer

    -- RESOLVE-side source watermark: the source-log position as of the last-resolved transaction, and what is stamped onto each replica record.
    -- Leads the durable watermark on the watchers, which advances only on apply, and for a block's covered position only on the adopt.
    last_resolved_src_msg_id: MessageId

    -- The last non-null external-source token seen on the resolve side.
    -- Carried onto a block boundary so that the persisted block holds a live resume point.
    last_resolved_ext_token: ExternalSourceToken?

    -- RESOLVE-side block gauge: rows resolved into the currently-open block.
    -- The boundary is cut off this rather than off live_index.is_full, which lags, reflecting only APPLIED transactions.
    -- Seeded from live_index.block_row_count; reset when a boundary is injected.
    rows_since_block: Integer

    -- A block cut is in progress: the boundary has been appended and its upload has not been read back.
    -- Resolution is PAUSED while true, so no transaction interleaves between the boundary and the adopt that closes its block, and the buffer of held records stays empty.
    -- True from promotion where the term inherits a block to produce, so such a term resolves nothing until it has adopted that block.
    block_in_progress: Boolean
}

variant Follower : LogProcessor {
    -- Reads the replica log — and is the sole replica-log consumer for this node — feeding the shared live index from messages that are all already durable.
}

entity TxResolver {
    -- The leader's transaction resolver, opaque here: the queue of resolved-but-not-yet-applied transactions, the read-your-writes layering and the per-transaction durability handles are all live-index.allium's.
    -- db.allium does not `use` live-index.allium, so it names only what its own rules need.
    --
    -- It exists for the length of one leader term, seeded from the durable head. Absent while following.
    --
    -- RESOLVED head: the highest transaction resolved, and so staged.
    -- Drives resolution — the next external-source tx_id, system-time smoothing, read-your-writes.
    -- Leads the durable head by exactly the transactions staged but not yet applied.
    latest_completed_tx: TransactionKey?

    -- The queue itself is not modelled here.
    -- What db.allium relies on is the ORDER PROPERTY that makes the queue head predictable: resolution order = append order = read-back order, and terms are unique per leader.
    -- So an equal-term resolved transaction read back is always the matching head, which is why apply can import without re-reading the record.
    -- It is checked rather than assumed (see LeaderAppliesOwnTx).
}

entity TableCatalog {
    -- What one database knows about its tables on this node, and the block that knowledge is current to.
    -- The two are one value, replaced wholesale, so a reader sees a block index against that block's table metadata and never against the previous block's.
    --
    -- A block's per-table metadata folds in exactly once, on the adopt.
    -- The fold sums row counts, so folding the same block twice double-counts it, and that count is what the per-table block file carries.
    -- The produce computes the same fold locally to build those files and installs none of it.
    latest_block: Block?
    table_metadata: Map<trie_cat/TableRef, TableMetadata>

    -- Derived
    current_block_index: latest_block?.block_index
    boundary_replica_msg_id: latest_block?.boundary_replica_msg_id
    -- Seeds the fence's high-water, which is what makes it survive restart.
    boundary_term_id: latest_block?.term_id ?? 0
}

entity Watchers {
    -- The DURABLE watermarks for one database, plus the external-source resume token where it has one.
    -- Whichever role is live advances them only once a message has been applied.
    -- Clients await a tx_id to learn a transaction's result; a promotion awaits a source position.
    --
    -- A block's covered source position is reported on the ADOPT, by whichever role adopts the block: a node that has not recorded the block claims none of the position it covers, so a caller that flushes a block and then awaits this watermark is told of it once the flush is done.
    --
    -- An ingestion fault puts them into a failed state, and every subsequent await throws (see ProcessingErrorHaltsNode).
    latest_tx_id: MessageId
    latest_source_msg_id: MessageId
    latest_tx_result: TransactionResult?
    external_source_token: ExternalSourceToken?

    -- Monotonicity obligations, enforced as records are applied rather than expressible as state properties:
    --   a transaction notification must supply tx_id strictly greater than latest_tx_id;
    --   every notification must supply a source msg_id at or above latest_source_msg_id.
    --   At or above rather than strictly above: the upload closing a block can carry the same source position as the preceding transaction, where the block was triggered by the row gauge rather than by a flush.
}

external entity StorageFormat {
    -- This node's current storage format, as a version and an epoch.
    -- Every tries-added and block-uploaded message carries its own; a mismatch signals a storage upgrade in progress, and the message's tries are forwarded but not registered locally until this node upgrades.
    storage_version: Integer
    storage_epoch: Integer
}

---- Values

value MessageId {
    -- An epoch and an offset, ordered lexicographically.
    epoch: Integer
    offset: Integer
}

value ExternalSourceToken {
    -- An opaque resume token supplied by an external source, e.g. a Postgres LSN.
    -- Carried through a resolved transaction, a block boundary and a block upload on the replica log, tracked on the watchers, and persisted in the block, so that on restart the source can resume from the last durably persisted position.
}

value TransactionKey {
    -- tx_id is monotonically increasing, assigned by the source log or off the RESOLVED head for an external-source database.
    -- system_time is NOT monotonic — clients can set arbitrary system times — so ordering comparisons MUST use tx_id.
    tx_id: Integer
    system_time: Timestamp
}

value TransactionResult {
    -- The outcome of a resolved transaction, carried through the resolver's queue head and reported to the watchers on apply.
    tx_key: TransactionKey
    committed: Boolean
    error: Throwable?  -- present iff not committed
}

value Block {
    -- A persisted block in the object store.
    block_index: Integer
    latest_completed_tx: TransactionKey
    latest_processed_msg_id: MessageId           -- source log
    boundary_replica_msg_id: MessageId?          -- replica log: the position of this block's boundary
    external_source_token: ExternalSourceToken?  -- the last token seen at block time
    -- The term that APPENDED this block's upload.
    -- Persisting it is what makes the read-side fence survive a restart: the fence's high-water is seeded from here, so a leader claiming a regressed election counter is refused rather than silently ignored (see log-processor-lifecycle.allium, ClaimUnfenced).
    term_id: Integer
    -- Every table this block covers, and where each one's files live. See contract TableEntries.
    tables: List<TableEntry>
}

value TableEntry {
    -- A table's identity, and the directory under which its files live.
    oid: Integer
    table: trie_cat/TableRef
    slug: String
}

value PendingBlock {
    -- The state between a block boundary and the upload that closes it.
    -- Held by whichever role is live, which also holds the replica messages that arrive behind it, up to max_buffered_records.
    -- A held record has already met the fence, at its own position in the log: the hold sits below the fence, so what it stores has been admitted.
    -- Overflow is a fault (xtdb.indexer/follower-buffer-overflow) that the leader's block-cut pause keeps unreachable.
    -- Passed on at every role change, in both directions.
    boundary_msg_id: MessageId
    boundary_message: BlockBoundaryMessage
    buffered_records: List<ReplicaMessage>
    max_buffered_records: Integer
}

value Snapshot {
    as_of: TransactionKey
}

value TableMetadata {
    row_count: Integer?
}

value DbOp {
    -- A database-level side-effect carried within a resolved transaction.
    kind: Attach | Detach
}

variant Attach : DbOp {
    db_name: String
    config: DatabaseConfig
}

variant Detach : DbOp {
    db_name: String
}

---- Source Log Messages

-- Messages on the source log. Written by clients, by the leader (flush-block), and by the block upload and the compactor (tries-added).

entity SourceMessage {
    msg_id: MessageId
    log_timestamp: Timestamp
    kind: TxMessage | LegacyTxMessage | FlushBlockMessage | TriesAddedMessage
         | AttachDbMessage | DetachDbMessage
}

variant TxMessage : SourceMessage {
    -- Client-submitted transaction operations.
    system_time: Timestamp?
    default_tz: ZoneId               -- always present
    user: String?
    user_metadata: Bytes?
}

variant LegacyTxMessage : SourceMessage {
    -- A transaction written by an older node: the whole message is one opaque payload rather than a structured envelope.
    -- Still resolvable, so that a log written by an older node replays.
    payload: Bytes
}

variant FlushBlockMessage : SourceMessage {
    -- The leader sends this to itself to trigger a block flush on timeout, so that the cut goes through resolution in log order like any other boundary trigger.
    expected_block_idx: Integer?
}

variant TriesAddedMessage : SourceMessage {
    -- New tries available: L0 posted by a block upload, L1+ written by the compactor.
    -- The leader forwards these to the replica log.
    storage_version: Integer
    storage_epoch: Integer
    tries: List<trie_cat/TrieDetails>
}

variant AttachDbMessage : SourceMessage {
    db_name: String
    config: DatabaseConfig
}

variant DetachDbMessage : SourceMessage {
    db_name: String
}

---- Replica Log Messages

-- Messages on the replica log, written by the leader for its term.
-- Consumed by every node, the leader itself included on read-back, and by its own follower after a demote.

entity ReplicaMessage {
    msg_id: MessageId
    log_timestamp: Timestamp

    -- The leader term that produced this message; 0 on a record written before terms existed.
    -- Set by the message's CREATOR rather than by the append, and carried on the envelope so that every message kind is fenceable.
    -- This field is the whole fence: there is no fencing token and no write-time failure.
    term_id: Integer

    kind: ResolvedTxMessage | BlockBoundaryMessage | BlockUploadedMessage
         | TriesAddedMessage | TriesDeletedMessage | NoOpMessage
         | OversizedMessage
}

variant ResolvedTxMessage : ReplicaMessage {
    -- A resolved transaction: put, delete and erase events that require no database reads.
    -- An attach or detach result is carried as a database operation within it.
    -- For an external-source database it carries the resume token as at the transaction boundary.
    tx_id: MessageId                          -- the source-log position of the original message,
                                              -- or the resolver-assigned tx_id for an external-source database
    system_time: Timestamp                    -- the resolved system time
    committed: Boolean
    error: Throwable?                         -- the abort reason, null if committed
    db_op: DbOp?                              -- an attach or detach side-effect, if any
    table_data: Map<String, Bytes>            -- the resolved row data per table
    external_source_token: ExternalSourceToken?

    -- The leader's RESOLVE-side source watermark when this record was produced: for a source-log transaction its own position, for an external-source one the leader's current source-log position, so that followers keep their durable watermark in step between block boundaries.
    -- Null only on records written before the field existed, where readers fall back to the tx_id or to the tracked watermark.
    src_msg_id: MessageId?
}

variant BlockBoundaryMessage : ReplicaMessage {
    -- The leader signals that a block boundary has been reached.
    -- Injected in RESOLUTION order, off the resolve-side row gauge or on a successful flush CAS, so that it lands after this block's transactions and before the next block's.
    -- Whichever role reads it holds a pending block on it and holds the messages behind it until the matching upload; the leader that cut it also produces the block here.
    -- L0 tries are delivered in the subsequent upload rather than separately.
    block_index: Integer
    latest_processed_msg_id: MessageId           -- the resolve-side source watermark at block time
    external_source_token: ExternalSourceToken?
}

variant BlockUploadedMessage : ReplicaMessage {
    -- The block has been written to the object store.
    -- Carries the L0 tries, so that every node can update its trie catalog.
    -- The role holding the block adopts it on this and moves onto the next block.
    block_index: Integer
    latest_processed_msg_id: MessageId
    storage_version: Integer
    storage_epoch: Integer
    tries: List<trie_cat/TrieDetails>
    external_source_token: ExternalSourceToken?
}

variant TriesAddedMessage : ReplicaMessage {
    -- New tries available, forwarded by the leader from the source log.
    -- source_msg_id carries the original source-log position, so that staleness checking and the watchers advance correctly.
    storage_version: Integer
    storage_epoch: Integer
    tries: List<trie_cat/TrieDetails>
    source_msg_id: MessageId
}

variant TriesDeletedMessage : ReplicaMessage {
    -- Tries the leader's trie GC has deleted from the object store, published so that every node drops them from its in-memory catalog at the same logical position.
    -- Written only by the leader, and only once every file in the chunk is gone.
    -- Replay is always safe: the removal is idempotent and never stale.
    -- See: gc.allium.
    table_name: String
    trie_keys: Set<trie_cat/TrieKey>
}

variant NoOpMessage : ReplicaMessage {
    -- Carries no effect of its own, and is used for two things.
    --   1. A promotion's term claim. A new leader appends one stamped with its term to learn a known, CONSUMER-DELIVERABLE position: the log's latest-submitted position won't do, because it can include positions a consumer never delivers.
    --   2. Carrying the leader's source watermark forward where no other record would — a flush whose CAS fails, and which therefore finishes no block.
    src_msg_id: MessageId?   -- null when it is purely a term claim
}

variant OversizedMessage : ReplicaMessage {
    -- Stands in for a message the log declined for its size.
    -- The declined message's own encoding is in the object store and this names it, so the indirection is a property of the envelope rather than of any one kind: resolved transactions, tries-added and block-uploaded are all unbounded and all offload through it.
    -- Written by the leader only, and only on a refused append — the log is asked first, and this is what happens when it says no.
    -- term_id on the envelope is the DECLINED message's, so read-side fencing decides without fetching the payload.
    -- Readers resolve it as the record is read, ahead of both the pending-block hold and the staleness check: an offloaded upload closes a block, and one left unresolved would be held behind the block it closes.
    -- block_index is the block the standing-in record lands in — the open one, not the last completed — because block-gc.allium collects on it.
    storage_version: Integer
    storage_epoch: Integer
    block_index: Integer
    payload_id: String
}

---- Config

config {
    -- The worst-case interval over which an external source's upstream must retain its log, so the value bounds more than block cadence.
    flush_timeout: Duration = 15.minutes
    rows_per_block: Integer = 102400
}

---- Rules: Role Transitions

-- == Fencing ==
--
-- There is at most one CONFIRMED leader per database, enforced by a TERM on the read side.
-- There is no write-time fence.
--
-- Every replica record carries the term of the leader that created it.
-- The term pairs an operator-declared epoch with the elector's own counter, packed so that the epoch dominates the ordering, so plain numeric comparison is already lexicographic over the pair.
--
-- The fence is applied ONCE to every record, on this node's replica read and before either role acts on it: a record below the highest term seen so far is DISCARDED, and one at or above it folds that high-water upward and is handed on.
-- Records are read one at a time, handed to the live role and awaited, so the fence has a single caller at any moment.
-- The high-water belongs to the database on this node, seeded from the last persisted block, so the fence survives restart.
-- A discarded record still advances the read position, so a promotion's catch-up can never hang on a fenced record.
--
-- The fold belongs to the RECORD rather than to the role that acts on it, so it happens on admission, whichever role ends up taking the record.
--
-- An admitted record above the term a LEADER is leading has superseded that term — Raft's ReadIndex: a leader applies and acknowledges a write only once it has read that write back at its own term.
-- That leader is DEMOTED where the record is read, without acknowledging its outstanding writes, and the record is then handed to the follower that replaces it.
-- A follower leads no term, so nothing handed to one supersedes it.
--
-- Every record meets the fence at its own position in the log, the ones held behind an OPEN PENDING BLOCK included: the hold sits BELOW the fence, so a record is admitted before it is held, and the drain applies what the block was holding without consulting the fence again.
-- The block closes on the MATCH — block index, storage version and storage epoch, and never the term, which the fence has settled before the record reaches the match.
-- So an upload written by a term the log has moved past closes nothing: it is discarded like everything else that term wrote, and the block stays open until a term at or above the high-water uploads it again.
--
-- Two nodes may therefore briefly both believe themselves leader and both APPEND.
-- Only the newer term's writes are ever confirmed, and the older leader's are discarded, which is the property that matters — not single-writer-at-the-log.
--
-- The SOURCE log is not fenced.
-- Stale messages from a previous leader (a flush) or from the compactor (tries-added) may appear after a transition, and both are safe:
--   a flush is a CAS on the expected block index, so a stale one fails the check;
--   adding tries is idempotent, so a duplicate is a no-op.

-- == Transition Safety ==
--
-- The end of a term ABANDONS in-flight resolution rather than draining it.
-- A staged-but-not-yet-applied transaction was never visible to an external query, so discarding it costs nothing: the next leader re-reads it from the source log and re-resolves it.
-- What the end of a term MUST do instead is fail everything awaiting it — every staged external-source transaction's durability handle, the paused source batch, and whatever is still queued — because the symptom of missing one is a HANG.
--
-- So the live index still reflects a complete prefix of the replica log: it is only ever advanced by APPLY, one whole transaction at a time, and an abandoned transaction is one that was never applied.

-- == Leader Startup ==
--
-- A new leader can always reconstruct correct state from the last persisted block and the follower's in-memory state, which has been built by processing replica messages.
--
-- Claiming a replay target and awaiting it ensures the follower has fully caught up before its state is captured and it is stopped: the follower is the sole replica-log consumer and the leader consumes the source log instead, so records between the follower's position and the target would otherwise be silently skipped.
--
-- Where the follower had a pending block — a boundary read but no upload — the new leader produces that block under its OWN term, before its role is published, and adopts it once it reads an upload of that block back.
-- That upload is this term's own: an outgoing leader's upload of the same block sits below the high-water by the time it is read, and is discarded.
-- The block stays held across the cutover, and the records the follower was holding drain at the adopt.
--
-- Such a term resumes the source log at the last applied transaction, so it re-reads whatever the held boundary covered: the flush that triggered the cut, and nothing at all where the row gauge cut it, the boundary there carrying the preceding transaction's position.
-- Resolution is paused until the adopt, so the re-offered batch waits; and by the time the flush is re-resolved the adopt has advanced the current block index, so its CAS fails and it becomes a no-op.

-- == The block cycle ==
--
-- A block is filled, cut, produced and adopted, and the leader term that holds the cycle runs it for this term's own blocks and for the one a promotion inherited alike.
-- Resolution is armed only while filling, so nothing is resolved between a boundary and the adopt that closes its block.
-- Leader.rows_since_block and Leader.block_in_progress are the coarser view this spec's rules take of that cycle.
--
-- A cut is TWO operations — the block is PRODUCED on the boundary and ADOPTED on the confirmation coming back — which is also how a follower reads the same pair of messages.
--
-- PRODUCE, on reading the boundary back:
--   1. Finish the live index's block, yielding L0 tries — one per table that took rows in this block, and none at all for a table that took none.
--      A block cut with no transactions in it therefore writes no tries whatsoever.
--   2. Compute the trie layout those L0 tries imply, which is what the per-table block files record and what the trie catalog will hold once the adopt has added them.
--   3. Build the per-table block files and upload one for EVERY table the table catalog knows, not only the tables from step 1.
--      Block GC's per-table sweep depends on this — see block-gc.allium, EveryTableBlockAtCurrentIndex.
--   4. Build and persist the block file, carrying this term, which is what seeds a restarted node's fence, and stamp the database's last-upload time.
--   5. Append the upload to the replica log and await it.
--      This is the message the whole cluster closes the block on, this node included, so it is appended directly rather than behind whatever else is queued, a term ending in between dropping the rest.
--   The block, its L0 tries and its boundary are held from here until the adopt.
--   What is NOT installed here is the table catalog's metadata fold and the trie catalog's state, and the live index is not rolled — so a term dying between the two operations leaves the block RE-PRODUCIBLE, a second attempt at an index the catalog already holds being refused.
--   Table entries the produce DOES allocate, minting one for a table new in this block: the per-table file paths of step 3 and the block file of step 4 both record them, so they cannot wait for the adopt.
--   That allocation is what keeps re-producibility true rather than an exception to it: it is idempotent and carries no summed field, so a block produced twice mints nothing the second time and no value drifts.
--
-- ADOPT, on reading a confirmation of the held block back: see Adopting a block.
--   The role that produced the block holds it until here, because only the produce can build it; the tries and the log timestamp come off the confirming record, as a follower's do.
--   The records held behind the boundary are applied once the adopt has returned.
--   Nothing may apply a transaction between the produce's step 1 and the roll the adopt ends on: the live tables have been snapshotted into L0 and are about to be cleared, so a transaction applied in between is written nowhere.
--   The pending-block hold is what holds a record arriving in that window, and the resolution pause is what keeps the set of held records small.
--
-- The MATCH that closes a block is on block index, storage version and storage epoch, and never on term — the term having been settled by the fence before the record reaches the match.
--
-- The producing term is not always the boundary's: a promotion produces the previous leader's pending block.
-- An upload carries the term of whoever appends it, because a leader confirms a write only on reading it back at its own term.
-- Two terms can therefore produce one block index, where the term that cut the boundary also produced it before ending: one boundary over one prefix, covering one source position.
--
-- At most ONE of those two uploads is admitted on any reader, and the log's order is what decides which.
-- Where the outgoing leader's upload PRECEDES the incoming claim, every reader closes the block on it before reading the claim — the promoting node included, which therefore inherits no block and produces nothing.
-- Where it FOLLOWS the claim, every reader has folded the newer term by the time it arrives and discards it, and the block closes on the incoming term's own upload.
-- So a block a dead leader left open always closes, on one upload or the other, and never on both.

-- == Adopting a block ==
--
-- Adopting a block is ONE operation, run by whichever role holds the block.
-- It adds the block's L0 tries to the trie catalog as of the record's log timestamp, refreshes the table catalog onto the block, folding in the live index's block metadata, and rolls the live index onto the block behind it.
-- The metadata read precedes the roll, which clears the live tables it reads.
-- The leader and the follower run that one operation, not two matching sequences: what each supplies differently is the block itself — the leader passes the one its produce built and has held since, the follower one it reads from storage off the block index the boundary carried.
-- The tries and the log timestamp come off the record either way.
--
-- The block MUST be durable before it is adopted, because the adopt is what moves this node's catalog past it: a term that dies before adopting leaves the block re-producible, while a catalog past a block nobody wrote is past it for good.
-- It MUST run exactly once per block, the table catalog's refresh summing row counts.

-- == Staleness ==
--
-- A replica message whose effect has already been applied is skipped.
-- Each kind is compared against the watermark that actually records its effect, rather than against a single source-log position:
--   resolved transaction: stale if its tx_id is at or below watchers.latest_tx_id
--   tries added:          stale if its source position is at or below watchers.latest_source_msg_id
--   block boundary:       stale if its block index is at or below table_catalog.current_block_index
--   block uploaded:       stale if its block index is at or below table_catalog.current_block_index
--   no-op:                stale if it carries a source position at or behind watchers.latest_source_msg_id;
--                         a bare term claim is never stale
--   tries deleted:        never stale — the removal is idempotent, so replay is a no-op
-- A stale message is skipped entirely before dispatch, which is what replaces per-kind idempotency checks within each handler.
--
-- The term fence is a SEPARATE check, run first and on the read (see Fencing): a record the fence discards reaches neither the pending-block hold nor the staleness comparison.
-- The hold runs next, so the upload that closes a held block is MATCHED there rather than compared: the adopt is what advances this node's catalog past the block.
-- An upload reaching the comparison with no block in flight is one this node has already adopted, and is stale by block index; an index the catalog has NOT reached is a fault.

rule TransitionToLeader {
    -- db.allium's view of a promotion: what it means for THIS database's pipeline.
    -- log-processor-lifecycle.allium owns the ordered steps and the recovery path; they are not duplicated here.
    when: PromotionRequested(database, term)
    requires: log_processor.mode = Follower

    ensures:
        -- Claim the term: append a no-op stamped with it to learn a known, consumer-deliverable replay target.
        let replay_target = NoOpMessage.created(term_id: term, src_msg_id: null)
                                appended to database.replica_log

        -- Wait for this node's replica read to finish with the claim.
        -- This waits on the READ position, which advances once per record whatever the live role did with it, not on the durable watermarks, which a fenced or held record does not advance.
        -- Where the read has stopped, the await FAILS with its cause, the claim already passed or not: a term whose replica log nothing is reading would never read its own writes back, so a promotion behind a stopped read is refused here.
        AwaitReplicaPosition(log_processor, replay_target)

        -- Refuse a term the replica log has already moved past.
        -- Our own claim has just been read back, so the high-water is expected to BE this term; a higher one is another leader's, and leading under a fenced term would index nothing.
        -- Fails with xtdb/leader-term-fenced, naming the epoch to raise.
        -- Checked here — after the catch-up, before the follower stops — so that a refusal takes the recovery path with the follower still alive.
        CheckTermUnfenced(log_processor, term)

        -- Stop following. No further processing after this point.
        FollowerStopped(log_processor)

        -- Capture the follower's pending block, where it saw a boundary but no upload before the replay target.
        -- The read follows the stop: the follower goes on applying records until then, and one of them may be the upload that closes this very block.
        let pending_block = log_processor.pending_block

        -- Refuse a cutover the log has already moved past, before anything is built or appended.
        -- The same check as above, asked again because its answer goes stale: it is given while the follower is still live, and the read goes on folding every record it admits until that follower stops — the ones it handed the follower to hold included.
        -- A high-water above this term means a newer leader, and the promotion RESIGNS — reported the way a leader term reports its own supersession rather than as an ingestion fault, so it leaves the watchers alone.
        if log_processor.max_leader_term > term:
            LeaderSuperseded(resolver: tx_resolver)

        -- Take the leader role.
        -- It resumes the SOURCE log from the durable watermark, and reads the REPLICA log back from the replay target.
        -- Its resolver is seeded at the durable head, and its block gauge from the rows already in the open block, so it cuts this block where the previous leader would have.
        log_processor.mode = Leader.created(
            -- The database's own source, borrowed for the length of this term, so this leader is externally fed iff the database is.
            external_source: database.external_source,
            leader_term: term,
            last_resolved_src_msg_id: watchers.latest_source_msg_id,
            last_resolved_ext_token: watchers.external_source_token,
            rows_since_block: live_index.block_row_count,
            -- True where this term inherits a block to produce: resolution stays paused until it has adopted it.
            block_in_progress: pending_block != null
        )
        TxResolver.created(
            latest_completed_tx: live_index.latest_completed_tx
        )

        -- The new leader produces the block the outgoing one left behind, before it reads a source message.
        --   The block's data is already in the live index, applied before the boundary, but the block file was never persisted.
        --   The upload is appended under the INCOMING term, because a leader confirms a write only on reading it back at its own term.
        --   The block stays held. This term adopts it — and applies the records the follower was holding — on reading that upload back (LeaderAdoptsOwnBlock), which is after the role above is published and its replica read-back running.
        --   A term that ends in between hands the block on, and the role that takes it closes it on the same message this term was waiting for.
        if pending_block != null:
            ProduceBlock(log_processor, pending_block)

        SourceDeliveryResumed(after: watchers.latest_source_msg_id)

    @guidance
        -- ReplayEquivalence: the live index state after a promotion is identical regardless of how much work the follower had already done.
}

rule TransitionToFollower {
    when: DemoteRequested(database)
    requires: log_processor.mode = Leader

    ensures:
        -- The term ends. Everything awaiting it is failed, and every staged-but-not-yet-applied transaction is discarded — they were never visible to an external query, and the next leader re-reads them from the source log and re-resolves them.
        -- The durable live index is untouched (live-index.allium, ResolverEnds).
        LeaderTermEnded(log_processor)
        if tx_resolver != null:
            ResolverExited(tx_resolver)

        -- A block this term produced and has not adopted is carried to the new follower, which closes it on the same message this term was waiting for.
        -- It is read after the term has ended, as a promotion reads the follower's: the term goes on applying records until then, and one of them may be the upload that closes this very block.
        -- Ending the term mid-boundary leaves no block here at all: that record's apply is incomplete, so it is offered again and the follower opens the block itself, as on any other boundary.
        -- The follower continues from this node's replica position rather than being seeded with one: the read spans the role change.
        log_processor.mode = Follower.created()
}

---- Rules: Transaction Submission

rule ClientSubmitsTransaction {
    -- A client submits a transaction: it is appended to the SOURCE log, which every node shares, so this succeeds whether or not this node happens to be the leader.
    when: ClientSubmitsTx(client, database, tx_ops, opts)

    requires: database.external_source = null

    ensures:
        let msg = TxMessage.created(
            system_time: opts.system_time,
            default_tz: opts.default_tz,
            user: opts.user,
            user_metadata: opts.user_metadata
        )
        msg appended to database.source_log
}

rule ClientSubmitsTransactionToExternalSourceDb {
    -- Rejected: submit is not a valid entry point for an external-source database, which assigns its own tx_id sequence off the resolved head and accepts transactions only through its source.
    -- Decided from the DATABASE's configuration, not this node's role.
    when: ClientSubmitsTx(client, database, tx_ops, opts)

    requires: database.external_source != null

    ensures:
        SubmitTxRejected(
            error_code: `xtdb/submit-tx-to-external-source-db`,
            db_name: database.name
        )
}

---- Rules: Leader Mode — resolve side

-- The leader resolves from four sources: the source log, the external source, the trie GC's publish step, and the resume of a paused block cut.
-- All but the apply of a read-back record are SUSPENDED while block_in_progress — the block-cut pause.
--
-- Resolving a transaction stages it on the resolver's queue, advancing the RESOLVED head so that later transactions read-your-writes against it, and appends it.
-- Nothing here touches the durable live index and nothing here advances the watchers: both follow from apply, on read-back.
-- Resolution therefore runs AHEAD of durability.
--
-- At the start of each source batch the leader checks the block flush timeout.
-- A batch that a block cut pauses part-way is set aside and resumed after the upload, and is reported complete only once fully drained, so that source delivery stays in step with resolution.

rule LeaderProcessesTx {
    -- Covers a current and a legacy transaction message alike: they differ only in how the payload decodes, which is below this spec's abstraction level.
    when: SourceTxMessageReceived(msg)
    requires: log_processor.mode = Leader

    ensures:
        -- Resolve and stage on the resolver's queue, advancing the RESOLVED head.
        -- An aborted transaction is staged too, and still consumes a tx_id (live-index.allium, AbortTransaction).
        let resolved = ResolveTx(tx_resolver, msg)
        AppendTx(resolved, src_msg_id: msg.msg_id)
}

rule LeaderProcessesAttachDb {
    -- The leader asks whether the attach would be refused, and brings its own set into line when it reads the record back, as every other node does.
    -- A term superseded between resolving an attach and reading it back has therefore attached nothing.
    --
    -- The verdict accounts for what this term has already resolved as well as for the catalog, which shows the state before every database operation still queued on the resolver.
    -- The last one queued for a name says what will be there when this attach applies; with none, the catalog's own state stands.
    -- This is the layering a resolving transaction reads its own queued predecessors through, turned on the catalog rather than on tables.
    --
    -- A caller fault is resolved as a FAILED operation rather than propagating: the transaction aborts with that error, and the log carries the abort.
    when: SourceAttachDbMessageReceived(msg)
    requires: log_processor.mode = Leader

    ensures:
        let error = CheckCanAttach(msg.db_name, msg.config)
        let resolved =
            if error = null:
                ResolveDbOp(tx_resolver, msg, Attach{db_name: msg.db_name, config: msg.config})
            else:
                ResolveFailedDbOp(tx_resolver, msg, error)
        AppendTx(resolved, src_msg_id: msg.msg_id)
}

rule LeaderProcessesDetachDb {
    -- Mirror of LeaderProcessesAttachDb.
    when: SourceDetachDbMessageReceived(msg)
    requires: log_processor.mode = Leader

    ensures:
        let error = CheckCanDetach(msg.db_name)
        let resolved =
            if error = null:
                ResolveDbOp(tx_resolver, msg, Detach{db_name: msg.db_name})
            else:
                ResolveFailedDbOp(tx_resolver, msg, error)
        AppendTx(resolved, src_msg_id: msg.msg_id)
}

rule AppendTx {
    -- Hand a freshly-resolved transaction on to be appended, advance the resolve-side watermark, and cut a block if this transaction filled one.
    --
    -- src_msg_id is the source-log position this transaction sits at: a source-log transaction advances it; an external-source one passes the current position back, having none of its own.
    -- Either way it is what gets STAMPED on the transaction's replica record — see the header's note on the two watermarks.
    when: AppendTx(resolved, src_msg_id)

    requires: log_processor.mode = Leader

    ensures:
        log_processor.last_resolved_src_msg_id = src_msg_id
        log_processor.rows_since_block = log_processor.rows_since_block + resolved.row_count
        AppendReplicaMessage(resolved)

        if log_processor.rows_since_block >= config.rows_per_block:
            LeaderCutsBlock(log_processor.last_resolved_src_msg_id, log_processor.last_resolved_ext_token)
}

rule LeaderCutsBlock {
    -- Inject the boundary in RESOLUTION order, so that it lands after this block's transactions and before the next block's, and PAUSE resolution until it is read back and its block uploaded.
    -- The pause is what keeps the set of records held behind an open block empty.
    -- Deliberately the status quo — block double-buffering is separate work (#4495).
    --
    -- The same event as live-index.allium's CutBlock, from the db side: this is where the boundary MESSAGE is built, because its fields come from here — the next block index off the table catalog, the resolve-side source watermark, the resume token — and live-index holds neither the watermark nor the external source.
    --
    -- One rule, not one per leader kind: the token is whatever the resolve side last saw, null throughout for a client-driven leader, so there is nothing to branch on.
    -- Reading the token from the WATCHERS would be wrong here — they advance on apply, which lags this cut.
    when: LeaderCutsBlock(latest_processed_msg_id, external_source_token)

    requires: log_processor.mode = Leader

    let block_index = (table_catalog.current_block_index ?? -1) + 1

    ensures:
        AppendReplicaMessage(BlockBoundaryMessage.created(
            block_index: block_index,
            latest_processed_msg_id: latest_processed_msg_id,
            external_source_token: external_source_token
        ))
        log_processor.block_in_progress = true
        log_processor.rows_since_block = 0
}

rule LeaderProcessesFlushBlock {
    -- A source-log coordination message, resolved like any other.
    when: SourceFlushBlockMessageReceived(msg)
    requires: log_processor.mode = Leader

    ensures:
        -- CAS check: cut the block only where the expected index matches.
        if msg.expected_block_idx != null
           and msg.expected_block_idx = (table_catalog.current_block_index ?? -1):
            LeaderCutsBlock(msg.msg_id, log_processor.last_resolved_ext_token)
        else:
            -- A stale flush, or one with no expected index: nothing to cut, but the source watermark still has to reach the followers and no other record would carry it.
            AppendReplicaMessage(NoOpMessage.created(src_msg_id: msg.msg_id))

        log_processor.last_resolved_src_msg_id = msg.msg_id
}

rule LeaderProcessesTriesAdded {
    -- L0 from a block upload, L1+ from the compactor, forwarded to the replica log — where every node applies it to its own trie catalog, this one included (LeaderAppliesOwnTriesAdded).
    when: SourceTriesAddedMessageReceived(msg)
    requires: log_processor.mode = Leader

    ensures:
        -- Forwarded whatever its storage version: every node guards the add itself, and the message is also what carries the source watermark forward.
        AppendReplicaMessage(TriesAddedMessage.created(
            storage_version: msg.storage_version,
            storage_epoch: msg.storage_epoch,
            tries: msg.tries,
            source_msg_id: msg.msg_id
        ))

        log_processor.last_resolved_src_msg_id = msg.msg_id
}

-- The trie GC's publish step is a THIRD resolve-side source, alongside the source log and the external source: the GC hands the removal to the leader and awaits it rather than appending itself.
-- gc.allium owns that rule, including the ordering the append and the removal it publishes have to keep.
-- Two facts belong here, being properties of this pipeline rather than of the GC:
--   the GC's source is SUSPENDED while block_in_progress, so a tries-deleted can never be appended between a boundary and its upload;
--   its message is appended like any other, and the removal it carries is applied on the leader's own read-back (LeaderAppliesOwnTriesDeleted), which is where the chunk awaiting it is released.

---- Rules: Leader Mode — append

rule AppendReplicaMessage {
    -- Append the message to the replica log, in resolution order, STAMPED with this leader's term.
    --
    -- Nothing here is atomic across messages and nothing needs to be: a superseded leader is fenced by the term its records carry, checked when it reads them back, not by an append that fails.
    -- An append fault ends the term.
    when: AppendReplicaMessage(msg)

    requires: log_processor.mode = Leader

    ensures:
        msg.term_id = log_processor.leader_term
        msg appended to database.replica_log
}

---- Rules: Leader Mode — read back and apply

-- The leader is fed by this node's replica read, from the position its promotion claimed, separately from whatever drives leader election so that the two do not interfere.
-- This is where a committed transaction becomes queryable and where an external caller's durability handle completes: confirmed durably replicated AND confirmed unfenced.

rule LeaderAppliesRecord {
    -- Apply one record read back off our own replica log, fenced by TERM.
    -- The record carries this term's own term: terms are unique per leader, the fence discards anything below it and demotes this term on anything above it, so there is nothing left here to compare.
    when: HandleReplicaMessage(record)
    requires: log_processor.mode = Leader

    ensures:
        if log_processor.pending_block != null:
            -- The upload this term is waiting for is matched here, on block index, storage version and storage epoch and never on term, which the fence has settled already.
            if record.kind = BlockUploadedMessage
               and record.block_index = log_processor.pending_block.boundary_message.block_index
               and record.storage_version = storage_format.storage_version
               and record.storage_epoch = storage_format.storage_epoch:
                AdoptOwnBlock(record)
            else:
                log_processor.pending_block.buffered_records =
                    log_processor.pending_block.buffered_records.append(record)
        else:
            ApplyOwnRecord(record)
}

rule LeaderAppliesOwnTx {
    -- The leader reads its own staged transaction back at its own term and IMPORTS it, from the resolver's QUEUE HEAD.
    -- The head still holds the transaction's rows, so nothing is re-read from the replica record; that record's only job was to confirm durability and term.
    -- A head whose tx_id doesn't match the record just read back is a fault.
    --
    -- This is the point at which the transaction becomes queryable: the DURABLE head advances here, the watchers advance here, and an external caller's durability handle completes here — after, not before, the transaction is durable.
    --
    -- The database operation it carries is applied here, as a follower applies it on its own read — before the durability handle completes, so a caller that submitted an attach can use the database the moment its transaction returns.
    when: ApplyOwnRecord(record)
    requires: record.kind = ResolvedTxMessage

    -- Take the head FIRST, then check it.
    -- Taking it first is what makes the failure path safe: once off the queue, the end of the term can no longer reach it, so this rule owns failing its durability handle on ANY throw here, the tx_id mismatch included.
    let head = TakeResolvedHead(tx_resolver)
    requires: head.tx_key.tx_id = record.tx_id

    ensures:
        -- Everything reported below comes from the HEAD, not from the record: the head still holds the transaction's result, watermark stamp and token, which is exactly why the record needs no re-reading.
        -- The two agree by construction — the head is what was stamped onto the record at append time.
        ApplyResolvedTx(tx_resolver, head)
        if record.committed and record.db_op != null:
            ApplyDbOpToCatalog(database.catalog, record.db_op)
        watchers.notifyTx(head.tx_result, head.src_msg_id, head.external_source_token)
}

rule LeaderUploadsBlockOnBoundary {
    -- The leader reads its OWN boundary back and PRODUCES the block (see The block cycle).
    -- By log order the durable live index now holds exactly this block's transactions and no more — which is precisely what the block-cut pause buys: nothing was resolved and applied between the boundary and here, so a durable L0 block never contains rows from a later block.
    --
    -- The block is held from here, and the adopt waits for an upload of it to come back (LeaderAdoptsOwnBlock).
    when: ApplyOwnRecord(record)
    requires: record.kind = BlockBoundaryMessage

    ensures:
        log_processor.pending_block = PendingBlock(
            boundary_msg_id: record.msg_id,
            boundary_message: record
        )
        ProduceBlock(log_processor, log_processor.pending_block)
}

rule LeaderAdoptsOwnBlock {
    -- The leader reads an upload confirming the block it holds and ADOPTS that block (see Adopting a block), then applies the records held behind the boundary.
    -- It runs the adopt a follower runs, supplying the block its own produce built and has held since — until here nothing on this node has moved past the block.
    -- The upload adopted is this term's own: an upload of that block index from a term the log has moved past is discarded before it reaches here (see Fencing).
    -- The held records go back through the leader's own apply, BELOW the fence they met on the way in, so a boundary among them produces the next block there and the records behind that one are held again.
    when: AdoptOwnBlock(record)

    ensures:
        AdoptBlock(record)

        -- The block is durable and this node is on it.
        -- Announcing that as its own event is what lets an external source confirm its upstream position without reaching into the block cycle.
        BlockBecameDurable(database, record.external_source_token)

        watchers.notifyMsg(record.latest_processed_msg_id)

        -- A block boundary is the only moment new garbage can appear.
        BlockGcSignalled()
        TrieGcSignalled()

        -- Resolution resumes, picking up any source batch set aside.
        log_processor.block_in_progress = false
        ResolutionResumed()

        let buffered = log_processor.pending_block.buffered_records
        log_processor.pending_block = null
        for held in buffered:
            HandleReplicaMessage(held)
}

rule LeaderFaultsOnUnmatchedBlockUpload {
    -- An upload reaching a leader's apply with no block in flight is a FAULT, and nothing explains it: this node holds no boundary for that block, and the fence admitted the record at or above this term.
    -- Unreachable on three counts: this term's own upload is matched against the block it holds, one from a term the log has moved past is discarded, and one from a higher term demotes this term before the record reaches a role at all.
    when: ApplyOwnRecord(record)
    requires: record.kind = BlockUploadedMessage

    ensures:
        ProcessingError(msg_id: record.msg_id, error: "BlockUploaded reached apply with no block in flight")
}

rule LeaderAppliesOwnTriesAdded {
    -- The leader reads its own forwarded tries-added back and registers the tries in its trie catalog, under the same storage guard every node applies and off the REPLICA record's log timestamp.
    -- By log order the add lands ahead of any boundary appended behind it, so the per-table block files a block upload writes snapshot a catalog carrying every add the log holds ahead of that boundary.
    -- The watermark advance follows the add: the compactor awaits that watermark and then recalculates its jobs off the catalog, so advancing it first would have the compactor re-select the job it has just published.
    when: ApplyOwnRecord(record)
    requires: record.kind = TriesAddedMessage

    ensures:
        if record.storage_version = storage_format.storage_version
           and record.storage_epoch = storage_format.storage_epoch:
            trie_cat/trie_catalog.addTries(record.tries, record.log_timestamp)

        watchers.notifyMsg(record.source_msg_id)
}

rule LeaderAppliesOwnTriesDeleted {
    -- The leader reads its own tries-deleted back and drops the keys from its trie catalog, taking them from the RECORD, so it applies exactly what every other node applies.
    -- The chunk awaiting the append is released here, so gc.allium's publish returns with the catalog already past the keys whose files it has deleted.
    -- The record confirms the OLDEST chunk still outstanding: every tries-deleted a leader reads back is its own term's append — a higher term supersedes it and a lower one is fenced — and the log hands its own appends back in order.
    when: ApplyOwnRecord(record)
    requires: record.kind = TriesDeletedMessage

    ensures:
        trie_cat/trie_catalog.deleteTries(record.table_name, record.trie_keys)
}

rule LeaderAppliesOwnNoOp {
    -- Nothing to apply but the durable watermark, which is exactly what makes it lag resolution — and only where the no-op carries one, a term claim having none.
    when: ApplyOwnRecord(record)
    requires: record.kind = NoOpMessage

    ensures:
        if record.src_msg_id != null:
            watchers.notifyMsg(record.src_msg_id)
}

---- Rules: Leader Flush Timeout

rule LeaderFlushTimeout {
    -- Checked at the start of each source batch: where the current block's latest tx_id has not advanced since the previous check after flush_timeout, and this transaction has not already been flushed, the leader writes a flush to the SOURCE log.
    -- To the source log, so that the cut goes through resolution in log order like any other boundary trigger, rather than being applied out of band.
    when: BlockFlushTimeoutElapsed(database)
    requires: log_processor.mode = Leader

    requires: live_index.has_data

    ensures:
        FlushBlockMessage.created(
            expected_block_idx: table_catalog.current_block_index ?? -1
        ) appended to database.source_log
}

---- Rules: External Source

-- An external-source leader does NOT take transactions from the source log.
-- The source is handed an indexing callback on assignment and feeds transactions in directly.
-- Flush and tries coordination still use the source log, so the leader also reads it — but transaction, attach and detach messages MUST NOT appear there, being rejected at submit time (ClientSubmitsTransactionToExternalSourceDb).

rule ExternalSourceAssigned {
    -- On assignment the external source receives an indexing callback and the resume token to start after.
    -- The token comes from the watchers, which reflect the replay of the replica log and so may be ahead of the last persisted block.
    -- A node that leads, is demoted, then leads again is assigned again on the same source, so state belonging to one assignment lives inside the assignment rather than on the source.
    when: PartitionsAssigned(database)
    requires: log_processor.mode = Leader
    requires: database.external_source != null

    ensures:
        ExternalSourceStarted(
            source: log_processor.external_source,
            after_token: watchers.external_source_token
        )
}

rule ExternalSourceIndexesTx {
    -- The per-transaction entry point. The source supplies an opaque resume token, an optional system time, and a writer that populates a transaction with the transformed row data.
    -- It may await the result or not; the indexing below is identical either way, and the difference is caller-side.
    --
    -- Unlike a client-driven transaction, tx_id here is assigned off the RESOLVED head, not off a source-log position and not off the durable head.
    -- system_time is smoothed strictly greater than the previous transaction's.
    --
    -- The caller's handle completes on READ-BACK (LeaderAppliesOwnTx), so it means durably replicated AND confirmed unfenced.
    -- Every path on which the transaction will never be applied MUST fail it instead, or the caller waits forever — see live-index.allium.
    --
    -- Backpressure is one transaction of lookahead plus the block-cut pause: while a block is in progress the external source is suspended, so a bursty source cannot grow the resolver's queue without bound.
    when: ExternalSourceIndexTx(external_source_token, system_time, writer)
    requires: log_processor.mode = Leader
    requires: database.external_source != null

    ensures:
        -- The stamped watermark MUST be the resolve-side one, not the watchers': a source transaction resolved and appended ahead of this one applies first and pushes the durable watermark past a stale stamp, at which point the watchers' monotonicity obligation is broken.
        let resolved = ResolveExternalSourceTx(
            tx_resolver, system_time, external_source_token, writer,
            src_msg_id: log_processor.last_resolved_src_msg_id
        )

        if external_source_token != null:
            log_processor.last_resolved_ext_token = external_source_token

        AppendTx(resolved, src_msg_id: log_processor.last_resolved_src_msg_id)
}

---- Rules: Replica Message Handling

-- A follower applies already-durable messages, so it imports straight into the durable live index and advances the watchers as it goes.
-- The TERM FENCE (see Fencing) runs on the read, above everything below: only admitted messages reach a role at all.
-- The pending-block hold runs next, while a block is open, either matching the upload that closes the block or holding the record; STALENESS (see Staleness) runs last, skipping a message whose effect is already applied.
-- So only unheld, non-stale messages reach the handlers below, and all of them have been admitted.

rule LogProcessorDispatchesRecord {
    -- This node's replica read: read one record, fence it, hand what it admits to whichever role is live, await it, advance.
    -- Reading one at a time and awaiting it is what leaves the term fence with a single caller.
    when: ReplicaMessageReceived(record)

    ensures:
        if record.term_id < log_processor.max_leader_term:
            -- Written by a leader the log has since moved past, so every reader discards it.
            RecordDiscarded(record)
        else:
            log_processor.max_leader_term = record.term_id

            -- A record above the term a leader is leading has superseded that term (see Fencing).
            -- The demote is the one a revoke runs, not a second path, so the record is offered to the follower that replaces the term rather than waiting for the elector to notice.
            if log_processor.mode = Leader and record.term_id > log_processor.leader_term:
                DemoteRequested(database)

            HandleReplicaMessage(record)

        -- One advance per record, whatever became of it — this is what a promotion's await waits on.
        -- A record the fence discarded or a role held advances it all the same, so a promotion's catch-up can't hang on one.
        -- A faulting apply does not advance it: it ends the read.
        log_processor.latest_replica_msg_id = record.msg_id
}

rule FollowerHoldsRecordBehindPendingBlock {
    -- A record that arrives while a block is open is STORED, and nothing else — see PendingBlock.
    -- Subscribes to the same dispatch event as HandleReplicaMessage and overrides it, as FollowerHandlesBlockUploaded does for the record that closes the block.
    when: HandleReplicaMessage(msg)
    requires: log_processor.mode = Follower
    requires: log_processor.pending_block != null
    requires: not (msg.kind = BlockUploadedMessage
                   and msg.block_index = log_processor.pending_block.boundary_message.block_index
                   and msg.storage_version = storage_format.storage_version
                   and msg.storage_epoch = storage_format.storage_epoch)

    ensures:
        log_processor.pending_block.buffered_records =
            log_processor.pending_block.buffered_records.append(msg)
}

rule HandleReplicaMessage {
    -- Applies an admitted message on a follower: the staleness check, and then the apply.
    -- The pending-block hold overrides this rule while a block is open (FollowerHoldsRecordBehindPendingBlock, FollowerHandlesBlockUploaded), and LeaderAppliesRecord takes the message where a leader is live.
    when: HandleReplicaMessage(msg)
    requires: log_processor.mode = Follower

    ensures:
        if msg.kind = ResolvedTxMessage:
            live_index.importTx(msg)
            if msg.committed:
                if msg.db_op.kind = Attach:
                    database.catalog.attach(msg.db_op.db_name, msg.db_op.config)
                if msg.db_op.kind = Detach:
                    database.catalog.detach(msg.db_op.db_name)
            -- The stamped position where present.
            -- On an older record it is absent, and the fallback differs by database: an external-source tx_id is an independent counter, so it says nothing about the source log and the tracked watermark has to stand in; a source-log tx_id IS a source-log position.
            let effective_src_msg_id =
                msg.src_msg_id
                    ?? (if database.external_source != null:
                            watchers.latest_source_msg_id
                        else: msg.tx_id)
            watchers.notifyTx(msg.result, effective_src_msg_id, msg.external_source_token)

        if msg.kind = TriesAddedMessage:
            if msg.storage_version = storage_format.storage_version
               and msg.storage_epoch = storage_format.storage_epoch:
                trie_cat/trie_catalog.addTries(msg.tries, msg.log_timestamp)
            watchers.notifyMsg(msg.source_msg_id)

        if msg.kind = TriesDeletedMessage:
            -- Idempotent removal, so unconditional: no storage guard and no staleness check.
            trie_cat/trie_catalog.deleteTries(msg.table_name, msg.trie_keys)

        if msg.kind = BlockBoundaryMessage:
            -- The follower opens a pending block. Every subsequent message except the matching upload is held by the hold above.
            log_processor.pending_block = PendingBlock(msg.msg_id, msg)

        if msg.kind = NoOpMessage and msg.src_msg_id != null:
            watchers.notifyMsg(msg.src_msg_id)
}

rule FollowerHandlesBlockUploaded {
    -- The follower holding a block reads the matching upload and runs the adopt a leader runs (see Adopting a block), supplying a block it reads from the object store.
    -- The block does not come from the message; the message is just a signal.
    -- Subscribes to the same dispatch event as HandleReplicaMessage, which has no upload branch of its own, and overrides it for the pending-block case.
    -- The record has already met the fence, on the read and ahead of the hold, so the match is all that is left to test (see Fencing).
    when: HandleReplicaMessage(msg)
    requires: msg.kind = BlockUploadedMessage
    requires: log_processor.mode = Follower
    requires: log_processor.pending_block != null
    requires: msg.block_index = log_processor.pending_block.boundary_message.block_index
    requires: msg.storage_version = storage_format.storage_version
    requires: msg.storage_epoch = storage_format.storage_epoch

    ensures:
        -- The adopt, over the block at that index read from the object store.
        AdoptBlock(msg)

        CompactorSignalled()

        -- The block's covered source position, ahead of the drain below: the held records carry later positions, so behind them this one would go backwards (see the watchers' monotonicity obligations).
        watchers.notifyMsg(msg.latest_processed_msg_id)

        -- Close the block, then apply what it was holding; their own notifications are what advance the watermarks past this position.
        -- They re-enter BELOW the fence, each having met it at its own position in the log on the way in.
        let buffered = log_processor.pending_block.buffered_records
        log_processor.pending_block = null
        for record in buffered:
            HandleReplicaMessage(record)
}

---- Rules: Compaction Orchestration

-- The compactor runs asynchronously, signalled after a block is adopted.
-- Job selection and the merge algorithm are compaction.allium's.
-- The compactor writes L1+ tries to the SOURCE log, and the leader forwards them to the replica log.

rule CompactorWakesUp {
    when: CompactorSignalled()

    let jobs = compaction/available_jobs(trie_cat/trie_catalog)

    ensures:
        for job in jobs:
            StartCompactionJob(job)
}

rule CompactionJobWritesOutput {
    when: StartCompactionJob(job)

    ensures:
        -- The data file is uploaded first; the meta file's presence is the completion marker.
        -- The tries go to the source log, and the leader forwards them to the replica log.
        TriesAddedMessage.created(tries: job.output_tries) appended to database.source_log
}

rule CompactionJobConfirmed {
    -- The tries-added has round-tripped through the log and is now in the trie catalog.
    -- Job selection naturally sees the updated catalog for the next cycle, so this rule asserts the event and adds no postconditions of its own.
    when: CompactionOutputVisible(job)
}

---- Rules: Query Serving

rule ClientOpensSnapshot {
    -- External queries open at the DURABLE head, never the resolved head.
    -- A query must never see a transaction that could vanish if the leader crashed before its replica-log write landed, nor one a newer-term leader superseded.
    when: ClientOpensSnapshot(client, database)

    ensures:
        Snapshot.created(
            as_of: live_index.latest_completed_tx
        )
}

rule ExternalSourceLearnsDurableExtent {
    -- Confirming a resume token tells the upstream it may discard everything behind it, so a confirmed position is a claim that we can recover without it.
    -- Only a durable block backs that claim: between apply and the next block the sole copies are the replica log and the leader's live index, and the upstream cannot re-send what we told it to drop.
    --
    -- So this token, and not the applied position, is the source's licence to confirm.
    -- It is also not the watchers' token, which is the one that may run ahead — replica-log replay advances it past block boundaries — which is why the token an assignment starts after is not a position we are entitled to confirm.
    --
    -- What a source does with the licence is its own rule: it may confirm further where the log above the extent demonstrably holds nothing of ours. See pgsrc.allium.
    --
    -- The gap this opens is the block cadence: a database whose blocks stop landing pins the upstream's log while ingestion and queries stay healthy.
    -- That is a monitoring obligation, not a correctness one (#5867).
    when: BlockBecameDurable(database, external_source_token)
    requires: log_processor.mode = Leader
    requires: database.external_source != null
    requires: external_source_token != null

    ensures:
        DurableExtentAdvanced(
            source: log_processor.external_source,
            token: external_source_token
        )
}

---- Rules: Error Handling

rule ProcessingErrorHaltsNode {
    -- A genuine ingestion fault: the watchers move to a failed state, and every subsequent await throws.
    --
    -- A SUPERSESSION is not one of these, and neither is an interruption of the term: see LeaderSuperseded.
    -- Poisoning the watchers on a routine leadership handover would surface it to queries as a failed database.
    when: ProcessingError(msg_id, error)

    ensures:
        watchers.notifyError(error)
        NodeMarkedUnhealthy()
}

---- Contracts

contract TableEntries {
    -- Obligations on the entries a Block carries: each table's identity, and where its files live.
    -- These are about an entry's history across successive blocks, so they cannot be expressed as single-snapshot invariants over entity state.

    @invariant SlugFixedWhenFirstRecorded
        -- A table's slug is chosen once, by the first block that records the table, and every later block carries it forward unchanged.

    @invariant OidFixedWhenTheTableIsFirstSeen
        -- A table's oid is allocated when the catalog first learns of the table, and every later block carries it forward unchanged.
        -- - The catalog learns of a table when a transaction writes to it, which is earlier than the block that first records it.

    @invariant EveryNodeAllocatesTheSameOid
        -- Two nodes reach the same oid for a table without it passing between them.
        -- - Allocation follows the order tables first appear in the log, and the tables of one transaction are allocated in name order, so replaying the same log is enough to agree.

    @invariant EveryTableTheBlockNamesHasAnEntry
        -- A block records an entry for each table it names, taking the entry from the preceding block wherever that block held one.

    @invariant UnrecordedTableTakesItsEscapedName
        -- A table a block does not record takes the escaped form of its name as its slug.
        -- - This is what a block carrying names alone resolves to, so the files its tables have already written stay where they are.
        -- - Oids for those tables are derived in name order, so every node resolving the same block resolves the same entries.

    @invariant SlugPrecedesTheFirstWrite
        -- A table's slug is fixed before anything is written under its path.
        -- - A live table takes its entry when the live index creates it, so the L0 trie it writes at the block boundary lands under the slug the block then records for it.

    @invariant OnlyARecordedTableHasABlockFile
        -- A per-table block file exists for exactly the tables the block records.
        -- - A table allocated but not yet written has an entry and no file.
}

contract ExternalSourceConfirmation {
    -- Obligations on an ExternalSource that confirms progress upstream.
    -- These are about the ordering of confirmations against block durability over time, so they cannot be expressed as single-snapshot invariants over entity state.

    @invariant ConfirmedPositionIsRecoverable
        -- Everything the upstream has been told it may discard is recoverable without it, so recovery is the last block plus the upstream resumed from the token that block carries — one artefact, not two.
        -- - Ordinarily this bounds the confirmed position by the last durable block's token.
        --   A source MAY confirm beyond it over a range that holds nothing of ours; that is the source's rule to state and to justify, per pgsrc.allium.

    @invariant ConfirmationNeverNamesAnUnappliedTransaction
        -- No confirmed position covers a transaction that has not been applied.
        -- - Below the durable extent this holds by construction rather than by a check: a boundary is appended after the transactions it covers, so by the time a block is durable every transaction its token names has already been applied.
        --   The gate can therefore only hold confirmation back, never push it past apply.
        -- - Above the extent it holds because the only positions a source may confirm there are ones it has established name no transaction at all.

    @invariant ConfirmationIsLeaderOnly
        -- Only the leader for a database holds its ExternalSource, so only the leader confirms.
        -- A follower has no upstream connection and no position of its own, so a leadership handover cannot produce two nodes confirming different positions to the same upstream.
}

---- Actor Declarations

actor ClientActor {
    -- Minimal identification: any Client is a valid actor for the surfaces below.
    identified_by: Client where true
}

---- Surfaces

surface TransactionSubmission {
    facing caller: ClientActor

    context db: Database

    provides:
        ClientSubmitsTx(caller, db, tx_ops, opts)
            when db.external_source = null

    @guarantee OrderedProcessing
        -- Transactions are processed in the order they appear in the source log.

    @guarantee SubmitIsRoleAgnostic
        -- Submission appends to the shared source log, so it succeeds from any node regardless of which node leads this database.
        -- Whether the transaction has been INDEXED is a separate question, answered by awaiting its tx_id on the watchers.
}

surface QueryAccess {
    facing caller: ClientActor

    context db: Database

    provides:
        ClientOpensSnapshot(caller, db)

    @guarantee SnapshotIsolation
        -- A snapshot sees a consistent point-in-time view.
        -- It includes every transaction up to the durable head — those the leader has read BACK off its own replica log and applied.
        -- Resolved-but-not-yet-applied transactions are NOT visible, and neither are the writes of a leader a newer term superseded.
}

---- Deferred Specifications

deferred ResolveTx
  -- Resolve a source-log transaction — the put, delete and erase logic — stage it on the resolver's queue and advance the RESOLVED head.
  -- Covers the legacy payload form too.
  -- See: live-index.allium CommitTransaction / AbortTransaction
deferred ResolveExternalSourceTx
  -- Assign the next tx_id off the RESOLVED head, smooth the system time strictly greater than the previous transaction's, run the caller's writer against a fresh transaction, and stage the result with the caller's durability handle.
  -- See: live-index.allium CommitTransaction
deferred ResolveDbOp
  -- Resolve an attach or detach as a transaction carrying the database operation, for the catalog to be brought into line with when the record is read back.
deferred ResolveFailedDbOp
  -- Resolve an attach or detach that would be refused, as an ABORTED transaction carrying the caller fault that refused it.
deferred CheckCanAttach
  -- Whether an attach would be refused, and with which caller fault.
  -- Answered from the catalog's own records folded together with the database operations the resolver still holds: the last one queued for a name says what will be there when this attach applies, and with none the catalog's own records stand.
  -- Reaches its verdict without changing anything.
deferred CheckCanDetach
  -- Whether a detach would be refused, answered the same way.
deferred ApplyDbOpToCatalog
  -- Bring the catalog into line with a committed database operation, swallowing a caller fault: the transaction has already committed, so a node that cannot carry the instruction out holds none of a database the cluster agrees exists.
  -- See: database-lifecycle.allium AdmitInstructionCreatesEntry, RemoveBeginsTeardown
deferred TakeResolvedHead
  -- Remove and return the OLDEST staged transaction, passing responsibility for it to the caller, which must then import it, or fail its durability handle, on every path.
  -- Removal precedes validation deliberately; see LeaderAppliesOwnTx.
  -- See: live-index.allium ApplyResolvedTx
deferred ApplyResolvedTx
  -- Apply on read-back: import the head's rows into the durable live tables, advance the DURABLE head to it, and complete its durability handle.
  -- See: live-index.allium ApplyResolvedTx
deferred ProduceBlock
  -- Produce the block a boundary cut: finish the live index's block, build and upload the per-table block files and the block file under this term, and append the upload.
  -- See: The block cycle, above.
deferred AdoptBlock
  -- Adopt a block a confirmation names: add its L0 tries to the trie catalog, refresh the table catalog onto the block, and roll the live index onto the block behind it.
  -- See: Adopting a block, above.
deferred CheckTermUnfenced
  -- Refuse a claimed leader term the replica log has already moved past, naming the term epoch to raise (xtdb/leader-term-fenced).
  -- Refusing costs liveness only, never safety.
  -- See: log-processor-lifecycle.allium ClaimUnfenced
